WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:16.219
&gt;&gt; This follows on from the tradition I think
we started at the very first Board meeting,

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:22.640
when we were the Commissioning Board Authority
in those days, and we published a risk register.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:31.490
I think that was something that was quite
unusual, I think, and unique perhaps in public

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.820
service at the centre.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:39.309
We think through the process of transition
it served us really well; we were very honest

00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:46.129
about the challenges we faced, we were very
open about them, and that enabled us seriously

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:49.420
to deal with them and tackle them.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:53.539
We thought it was very important to carry
on in that vein as we have taken on all of

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:57.650
our responsibilities, and that is what this
Board Assurance Framework is about.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:04.239
I am very grateful to Ed and the Audit Committee
for the work they have done to help us develop

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:10.050
the methodology, and perhaps if I can just
speak very briefly about that methodology

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:11.160
first.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:18.270
What you have in front of you are 25 strategic
risks.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:25.830
They are, in the judgement of the Executive
Team, the most significant strategic risks

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:31.680
we are facing to the delivery of all our objectives,
what we want to achieve for patients and the

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:32.680
public.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.560
They come from two directions.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:43.090
The first is just looking at the difficulty
of the task and the objectives we are facing,

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:48.780
and being honest about the obstacles in the
way; then importantly, saying, 'What is it

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.110
we are doing about it, to mitigate those risks?'

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:57.080
However, there are also some risks, this second
source, which are external -- for example,

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:03.890
the financial environment that we are operating
in -- that we need to take account of, because

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:10.810
that really does impact on our ability, the
likelihood of us delivering everything we

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.099
want to deliver.

00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:15.959
That is how these have come together.

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:24.709
We are setting up a process to ensure that
this is not 25 set-in-stone, or else it can

00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:30.989
become just an end in itself.

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:37.049
Throughout our organisation, we are developing
an approach to understanding objectives and

00:02:37.049 --> 00:02:43.590
the risks that are attendant upon them, whether
that is in an area team in Cornwall or whether

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:49.719
it is part of the Support Centre in Leeds.

00:02:49.719 --> 00:02:55.379
There is the ability then for those risks
-- where they change or where something new

00:02:55.379 --> 00:03:03.349
and impactful comes along -- to be escalated
first of all to directorate level, but then

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:06.129
to the Executive Risk Committee.

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:13.999
That is an important role that we collectively
play in filtering some of those and understanding

00:03:13.999 --> 00:03:18.420
them: 'Yes, they are noteworthy, but we think
you should be able to manage that perfectly

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.420
well.'

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:25.459
Where we think there is something more significant
for us as an Executive Team, to really get

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:29.180
a grip on it, to give it the right senior
oversight; and where there is something we

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:34.420
believe is strategic, to bring it through
this process, to the attention of the Board.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.730
Therefore, these 25 will not stay still forever.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:46.569
Some, management can leave; some will be brought
to your attention as the escalation process

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:47.569
works.

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:49.989
The majority of the 25 are outward-facing,
as you will see.

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:57.040
They are about our confidence in delivering
the sort of quality, improvement and outcomes

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:04.180
we want for patients and the public, and relating
those to some of the actions we are taking.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:10.200
Then there are seven at the end that are about
our organisation still in transition: the

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:18.109
set of challenges that we still have to work
through to make sure that we are an excellent

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:22.990
organisation and therefore best fit to serve
patients and the public.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:33.980
I am very happy to take any comments from
colleagues, but also, Ed might want to give

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:36.410
his perspective from the Audit Committee on
the overall process.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:37.410
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.500
If I may call on Ed first?

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.120
&gt;&gt; I am happy to do so.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:48.280
Firstly, we should recognise that the NAO,
in their report on the transition, remarked

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.040
on the way in which we did this through the
transition and were positive in that approach.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.260
Therefore, I think adopting this in operations
is absolutely the right thing to do.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.200
It is a best practice.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:08.040
The transparency, though, which we reported
through these meetings, is also a best practice.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:15.000
You were right, and I will reinforce this:
it is only as good as the dynamic nature by

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.470
which it is used by the whole of the organisation
and, in particular, by the Board in monitoring

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:28.810
the way in which items move through this list,
either add to it or subtract from it, and

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:34.840
the actions that we are taking to mitigate
both the likelihood and the impact of the

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:35.840
risks.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.260
I am therefore hugely supportive of it.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:47.790
It has to be underpinned by a bubbling-up
of risks from across the organisation, and

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:55.950
it then forms a vital piece of the way in
which the internal audit function operates,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:06:03.210
the quality and clinical risk committee operates,
and the Board operates.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:11.250
I certainly commend it to this Board, Malcolm,
and I hope it becomes an essential Board and

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:15.340
leadership tool in the months and years ahead.

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:22.430
&gt;&gt; Again, it is really good to see, in a terrifying
way.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:30.270
It is good to see the one on procurement there;
I think just from observing from my day job,

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:37.030
I have seen people who want to improve services
being stymied by what I would regard as maybe

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:40.690
overcomplicated procurement rules (not imposed
by anybody in this room).

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:48.000
I am also wondering if there is another risk,
which is maybe still a transition risk, around

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.540
the relationship of the system with itself.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:57.950
I do not have a view as to whether Bournemouth
and Poole merging as a Foundation Trust is

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:03.240
or is not a good idea, but I do have a view
that if it is the right thing for outcomes,

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:09.270
another body saying, 'No, you cannot do that,'
for whatever duty that they have, is not the

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:10.270
right way to do it.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:15.180
I am wondering if there is another risk, which
is all the different components in the system

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.690
rubbing up against each other.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:21.250
We have not really worked out how to interact
with competition, with Monitor, with the EQC,

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:22.340
and so on.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:25.550
These are just thoughts really, rather than
comments, maybe.

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:31.810
&gt;&gt; I think that is a very good point.

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:36.760
Much of what we do will depend upon the quality
of our relationships with partner organisations,

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.430
and aligning our effort.

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:44.130
It is certainly something to take back to
the Executive Committee.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:47.860
&gt;&gt; It may not just be relationships; it may
be that people are doing what they have been

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:51.380
told to do in the new system, and that may
be directly contradictory to what we think

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:54.590
we have been told to do and how we are going
to work through that.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:55.590
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:57.910
It is the alignment of effort, yes.

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:05.540
&gt;&gt; I was just going to say, I think there
are some aspects of that in some of them.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:16.200
In 1 and 5, it is referred to, Ciarán, but
I do not know if you are looking for something

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:17.200
more specific.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.800
&gt;&gt; I had a meeting about two months ago with
the Chief Executive of the acute hospitals

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:27.820
in the southwest of England, which seems to
be a place where this is a particular issue.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.790
There were some extraordinary stories coming
out of that.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:35.760
Whether they were real or not, I am not absolutely
sure, particularly about the interplay between

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:46.779
Monitor and the OFT, and their focus on the
implications for competition as opposed to

00:08:46.779 --> 00:08:50.079
the implications for clinical standards, quality
or whatever.

00:08:50.079 --> 00:08:56.311
I think what we do not have here yet is quite
a mechanism to get that to bubble up through

00:08:56.311 --> 00:09:02.170
our system to enable it to happen, and I just
think that operational issue is something

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:06.269
we need to reflect on and think about how
we do it.

00:09:06.269 --> 00:09:07.269
I think it is a good point.

00:09:07.269 --> 00:09:08.269
&gt;&gt; Okay, thank you.

00:09:08.269 --> 00:09:10.329
Are we happy to approve the Board Assurance
Framework?

00:09:10.329 --> 00:09:11.329
Thank you.

00:09:11.329 --> 00:09:22.139
&gt;&gt; I have nothing really much to say; it is
more of a record that they are now on the

00:09:22.139 --> 00:09:28.029
website, they have been laid before Parliament,
they were laid in time, to schedule and with

00:09:28.029 --> 00:09:30.620
full accounting honours, as it were.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:36.240
I do not think the Board needs to know anything
more about them, unless there are any questions.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:40.680
&gt;&gt; On the basis that the Board does not need
to know anything more about them, we shall

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:45.160
move on, but thank you for getting them delivered
and laid before Parliament on time.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.370
That is an important formal step.

